### String Comparison & Concatenation Internals

In C, strings cannot be compared using `==` (which only compares pointer addresses). We use `strcmp()` from `<string.h>`:
- Returns `0` if strings are identical.
- Returns negative if `str1 < str2` lexicographically.
- Returns positive if `str1 > str2` lexicographically.

### Custom `strcmp` Implementation:
```c
int my_strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2) {
    while (*s1 && (*s1 == *s2)) {
        s1++;
        s2++;
    }
    return *(const unsigned char *)s1 - *(const unsigned char *)s2;
}
```

### Checking for Anagrams using Frequency Counts:
Two strings are anagrams if they contain the exact same characters in any order. Count character frequencies in a 256-element array:
```c
int count[256] = {0};
for (int i = 0; s1[i]; i++) count[(unsigned char)s1[i]]++;
for (int i = 0; s2[i]; i++) count[(unsigned char)s2[i]]--;
// Check if all counts return to zero
```

